    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d660">

                <pb facs="0519" n="511" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg511"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">394.115 Panay/151: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-19T13:00:00+09:00">December 19, 1937—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 19—2:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>657. We asked Yoshizawa this morning when replies to our signed note of December
                        14<anchor n="89" xml:id="d660-f1"/> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> of December 17<anchor n="90" xml:id="d660-f2"/> would be
                        <pb facs="0520" n="512" xml:id="pg512"/> made. He replied that the charge of
                    Japanese machine-gunning of the <hi rendition="#italic">Panay</hi> having been
                    made, the Japanese Government must first ascertain the facts. He confirmed that
                    an officer of the General Staff had left for Shanghai to investigate.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
